* verdict.i -- the shared verdict tail for every CONF68K test.** Pulled in with "use verdict.i" immediately after a test's own code, and* followed by "use report.i", which supplies the routines called below.* Order matters: this block must sit directly after the body so that the* body's "beq.s ispass" / "bra.s isfail" / "bcs.w setuperr" reach it.** This lived inline in all twelve tests until it was factored out here. That* was 37 identical lines twelve times over -- 444 lines that only mean* anything if they stay in lockstep, with nothing to keep them there. A fix* applied to eleven of them and missed on the twelfth would still build,* still run, and quietly format one RESULT line differently from the rest.* One copy cannot drift from itself.** The including test must define, before this point:*   EXPECT    the value the manual says the system should produce*   idstr     "RESULT tNN "*   passtr / failstr / errstr    the verdict words*   obsstr / expstr / descstr    the field labels and the description* and must leave the observed value in d6 before branching here.** ERROR is not FAIL: it means the test could not establish the situation it* set out to check (its own create/open/write step failed), so it has no* standing to report a verdict on the claim. d6 carries the error code that* stopped it.** setuperr is where every failed create/open/write lands. It falls straight* through into iserror rather than branching to it -- that branch was the one* far jump whose distance grew with the body, so removing it removes a* per-test range problem instead of solving it twelve times.setuperr moveq #0,d6 move.w d1,d6iserror lea errstr(pc),a5 bra.s reportisfail lea failstr(pc),a5 bra.s reportispass lea passtr(pc),a5report lea idstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr movea.l a5,a0 bsr putstr lea obsstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr move.l d6,d2 bsr put6dec lea expstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr move.l #EXPECT,d2 bsr put6dec lea descstr(pc),a0 bsr putstr bsr putcr bra.w finish